{
  "dom_id": "step-q278535",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)}$ is $x=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+2)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+2)(\\cancel{x-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$",
